speechtext:
Under the act which
created the department there was a fairly important change from the previous legislation which defined the functions of the old department of pensions and national health. The change was made when the department was set up in 1944, and it gave the Department of National Health and Welfare positive responsibility for measures which might improve the health of the civil servants of Canada.
In May, 1945, I was authorized by the government to announce a programme involving an expenditure of $328,000 for the first year to provide a number of health services for civil servants. While they might be subdivided under many heads, I believe I can put them under a few.
In the first place the programme involved an examination before entrance into the civil service. This included X-ray examination for tuberculosis;
Second, periodic examinations;
Third, emergency service for people who suddenly became ill while at work;
Fourth, the development of educational activities so as to bring to the attention of the civil service of Canada the need for taking proper health measures;
Fifth, provision of advice with regard to health matters. Without giving actual continuous service, we would provide expert counsellors who could advise as to the kind of doctor to be consulted, and as to what should be done under certain conditions and,
Sixth, the establishment of sanitary standards, health standards, standards regarding working conditions, lighting, lavatory accommodation, rest rooms, and the like, and the assurance of cooperation with other government departments in giving this service.
In other words, this programme was designed to give the civil servants of Canada the kind of service which any good employer now gives his employees. That was announced in May of this year, and only last week we were fortunate enough to secure the services of Lieutenant-Colonel ft. G. Ratz, to head this new division. This gentleman is experienced in public health work, has had experience along those lines in the army, and is now starting to get to work. Progress depends now on space and staff becoming available with which to proceed. I repeat that you have to give the civil service of Canada the same kind of health service which any good employer now gives to his employees.
